{
  "requirement": "No prompt, tool result, or tool error the harness gives the model MUST state a\nline number or line range of a project source, except within source text it\nquotes and where a tool error repeats a line number the model supplied. The\nharness MUST identify a source location by file and outline selector under\n{cite}`requirements.references.outline_selectors` instead.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.authoring.no_line_numbers`**\n1. No prompt, tool result or tool error given to the model may state a line number or range of a project source.\n2. Two exceptions are allowed: source text the harness quotes, and a tool error that repeats a line number the model supplied.\n3. Source locations must be given by file and outline selector, following `requirements.references.outline_selectors`.\n\n**Where line numbers could reach the model, and the test for each**\n- **`habeas` reads.**\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[reading_records_only_delivered_sources_and_their_terms]` asserts that an item read has no `line_start` or `line_end`.\n  - It also asserts that a line-range read at end of file has no `line_end` and returns a selector.\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[line_range_reads_record_the_innermost_covering_nodes]` asserts that every input from a line-range read, in a Rust file and in a plain-text file, has no `line_start` or `line_end`. Each one is identified by `fn[..]`, `mod[..]/fn[..]` or `line@..` selectors.\n  - Recorded inputs carry only the file, selector and hash.\n- **Mandatory inputs in the prompt.**\n  - `src/authoring/inputs.rs#mod[tests]/fn[anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs]` asserts exact JSON equality of the outline input as delivered: file, item, hash, kind, text. Any extra line field would fail it.\n  - `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[site_reviews_use_their_prompt_and_record_applies_and_the_site]` checks that the site prompt's subject message names the site by file and selector.\n  - In `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[sites_are_the_innermost_nodes_of_site_matches_and_violations_within_them]`, `matches_value` is compared exactly. It carries kind, query, item selector and violation text, with no lines.\n- **Search results.**\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[search_merges_context_windows_and_respects_ignore_rules]` asserts no `line_start` on entries and no `line` field on any line object. It compares the exact `{text, matched}` shape.\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[separate_windows_in_one_node_are_divided_by_an_elision_line]` compares exact entries: selector, kind, and text lines with an elision marker.\n  - `ast_grep` results and declared-query results go through the same `SearchNodes::entries` builder. I confirmed this in `structural` and `declared_query` in `src/authoring/tools.rs`.\n- **`outline` results.** `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs]` compares the exact JSON (selector, kind, name, children). It also asserts that plain-text entries are `line@` selectors with no `line_start`.\n- **`references` results.** `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` compares exact `{name, role, file, item}` sites. Sites use selectors such as `fn[work]`, with item null for an anchor-style markdown site. Reference sites exist internally with line numbers, and the test shows none reach the model.\n- **Tool errors.**\n  - `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_give_the_model_no_line_numbers_it_did_not_supply]` has two parts. It first checks that an out-of-range read repeats only the start line the model supplied. It then checks that a real parse error carries a `line N: ` position at the tool level but arrives at the model, through the full `assess` conversation, as `cannot parse` with that position removed.\n  - `src/authoring/line_numbers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_drop_the_source_line_of_a_parse_error]` covers each rewrite exactly. Parse-position and `at line N, column M` forms are dropped, and `file:N:` diagnostics become `file#:` (the root selector). A position elsewhere in the text, such as a \"first declared at\" file position, becomes `file#`. Both supplied forms, a single start line and a start-end range (\"is not an inclusive range\"), are preserved.\n  - `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[malformed_and_failed_reads_return_to_the_model_and_the_assessment_continues]` compares the delivered message to the filtered message for several failing reads.\n- **Static prompt and description text.** `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[submission_description_tests]/fn[submission_descriptions_explain_arguments_and_successful_completion]` asserts the submit-tool descriptions say to cite by \"outline selectors\" and \"never line numbers\". This covers the obligation to identify locations by file and selector.\n\n**Cited `requirements.references.outline_selectors`.** The outline, search, references and `habeas` tests assert selectors in the required forms (`kind[name]`, `kind@hash`, `line@hash`, nested `mod[inner]/fn[second]`). The root is identified as `file#`. No line numbers form part of a selector. Selector grammar itself is that requirement's own concern.\n\n**Result.** Every channel the requirement governs is exercised and asserted: prompts and mandatory inputs, each tool's results, and tool errors. Both the main case and the exceptions (supplied numbers repeated, source text quoted) are tested. I found no scenario the tests would accept that violates the requirement, and no gap, so none needs a `{fixme}`.",
  "references": "5f07d59d3d70ca6a503110d2a797133c2b9f1eafd54fb53e7ee2fb02587ec51a",
